WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.008 --> 00:00:12.060
My name is Spencer Claeys,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.500
and I specialize in deep cleaning

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.843
and removing layers of moss off of roofs.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.900
The area that you live in and the climate

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.268
definitely plays a huge factor

00:00:20.268 --> 00:00:22.890
into the amount of moss
that grows on roofs.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:23.885
Up here in the Pacific Northwest,

00:00:23.885 --> 00:00:26.280
we have a lot of moss because
there's a lot of rain,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.390
there's not a lot of sunlight,

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.950
and there's a lot of trees and stuff

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.292
that provide all that material
for the stuff to grow on.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:37.860
There's probably hundreds
of thousands of species.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.200
But generally, if it's green or black

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.725
or something living up there,

00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:43.235
it needs to be removed.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.026
So, here, this gutter-guard sponge

00:00:46.026 --> 00:00:47.384
was completely grown in,

00:00:47.384 --> 00:00:49.692
compacted with mold, moss.

00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:51.540
So we need to get it out,
put in some new gutter guards

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.160
so that the gutters can be flowing

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.577
and working as they should.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:56.520
The purpose of the gutter guard

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.590
is to keep stuff out of the gutters.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.020
This sponge got so grown
in, compacted and stuff,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.520
that we needed to remove it

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.650
and put in new gutter guards.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.860
The moss can get up in
between any crack or crevice

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.570
that you have up on the
roof and the gutters.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.370
It either grows bigger
and bigger and bigger,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.500
and then water can get
up in there as well, too,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.480
or in the winter too, when it freezes,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.640
it can freeze and expand,

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.740
really break that seal that your house

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.310
or roof typically has.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.200
So, moss really loves growing

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.270
especially on roofs
that have a lower pitch.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.470
When it has a higher pitch,

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.110
it has a harder time grabbing
onto a hold of the roof.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.870
But if it's a lower pitch
and it's a lot less steep,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.115
there can be quite a bit more moss

00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:36.985
because it has a lot easier time

00:01:36.985 --> 00:01:39.540
of getting in between the cracks there.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.456
And generally, you're going to see

00:01:40.456 --> 00:01:42.923
a lot more moss growth
on the northern side,

00:01:42.923 --> 00:01:44.700
where there's lack of sunlight.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.900
So that's really one
of the biggest factors.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.230
Moss has the easiest time growing

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.930
in between asphalt-shingle roofs.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.330
The moss can really get
up in there, grow, expand,

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.180
and break the seal of
the shingle for the roof,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.740
and water can get up in there.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.890
More moss, organic material,
and it can cause mold,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.390
and water can get up in
there and damage the roof.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.380
I'll generally start any cleaning process

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.880
with the leaf blower and the brush,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.590
getting all that organic material off

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.940
before I start treating it.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.690
So, when you see this
level of moss up on a roof,

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.550
there's almost no way around.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:18.660
You gotta get that moss off there.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.920
You gotta brush it off.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.660
Because even if you treat it, it's still,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.430
there's just so much
organic material up there

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.147
that the moss is going to
have a really easy time

00:02:26.147 --> 00:02:28.320
just regrowing. Once you get rid of it,

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.000
it's going to be so much
easier to treat the roof,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.310
keep it from coming back.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.050
I'll use a stiffer nylon brush.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.060
We really want to be
careful when we're brushing.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:36.960
If you brush too hard,

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.730
you can definitely damage the shingles.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:39.810
But if you're doing it the right way,

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.435
you're doing it gently, you
can just get rid of that moss

00:02:42.435 --> 00:02:44.340
by just gently kind of flicking it off.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.903
You can leaf-blow it off later.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.330
So, every roof is different.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.820
It really depends on how
established the moss is,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.461
how much moss, what type of moss,

00:02:52.461 --> 00:02:54.402
if there's a lot of
trees. That really affects

00:02:54.402 --> 00:02:56.570
the amount of pressure that I
use when I'm brushing it off.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.320
Here, I'm using a stick
because the moss is so grown in

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.990
that the brush can't even really
get in between the cracks.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.000
And I'll generally
alternate between the stick

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.710
and the brush, depending
on the moss level,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.620
depending on what gets
it off the quickest.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.260
I'll follow the leaf blowing
with another hand-brushing,

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.000
and this will get everything else

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.260
that the leaf blower didn't take care of.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.910
It'll get it loose, and then
we can blow it off later.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.750
Once all the moss is gone
and the roof is clean,

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.670
we can begin the treatment process.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.650
When you're treating a
roof, our main purpose

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.860
is to raise the alkalinity,
so raise the pH.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.450
And you can do that
with laundry detergent,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.083
you can do that with zinc powder,

00:03:33.083 --> 00:03:35.283
and also baking soda you could use.

00:03:35.283 --> 00:03:38.250
Sodium hypochlorite, which is like bleach.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.200
And with the treatment,
it's usually a combination

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.110
of different chemicals that
I use that raises the pH

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:44.640
and then makes it really hard for the moss

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.760
to get established and
come back in the future.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.020
When you see me spraying up there,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.900
that's usually the sodium
hypochlorite, basically bleach,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.340
killing everything up there,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.380
and then the moss-preventative powder.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.060
When I apply the treatment
powder up on the roof,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.950
I really want to be hitting
the peaks really hard,

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.980
because that's going to flow
down the roof eventually.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.400
Ledges, gaps, seams, places
around the windows as well.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.550
You want to put a little bit
extra sometimes down there.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.041
The size of the roof really determines

00:04:10.041 --> 00:04:11.174
the amount of powder and treatment

00:04:11.174 --> 00:04:12.630
I'm going to need to put up on the roof.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.100
When you put the treatment powder on,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:14.933
you can leave it on there.

00:04:14.933 --> 00:04:15.870
You don't really need to touch it.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.940
The rain is going to take
care of the rest for you.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.730
It's going to wash down
over the next couple weeks.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.440
Most cleanings that I do are roofs

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.870
that are at least five, 10 years old.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.280
Because moss is so damaging,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.510
I always recommend homeowners

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.670
get their roofs cleaned
and treated every year

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.433
to uphold the integrity and
longevity of their roof.

